WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:14.546
Several hours had passed since the train had left Fix and Aouda at Fort Kearney station.

2
00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:16.747
The weather was dismal.

3
00:00:17.627 --> 00:00:19.808
Fix sat motionless on a bench in the station.

4
00:00:20.649 --> 00:00:26.111
Aouda, despite the bitter cold, kept stepping outside, walking to the end of the platform and peering through

5
00:00:26.131 --> 00:00:27.292
the heavily falling snow.

6
00:00:28.948 --> 00:00:32.389
She would return chilled and remain inside only long enough to warm up.

7
00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:36.350
Then she would exit again, looking for any sign of the rescue party.

8
00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.371
The commander of the fort waited with them, careful to hide his worry.

9
00:00:41.471 --> 00:00:44.612
Night fell, but the little band of rescuers still did not return.

10
00:00:45.813 --> 00:00:49.213
As the falling snow diminished, the air became intensely cold.

11
00:00:50.034 --> 00:00:50.774
Where could they be?

12
00:00:51.614 --> 00:00:52.674
Had they found the Indians?

13
00:00:53.315 --> 00:00:56.015
Were they fighting with them, or were they still wandering in the cold?

14
00:00:56.876 --> 00:00:58.036
Aouda suffered through the night.

15
00:00:58.924 --> 00:01:02.688
Her heart was filled with anguish, and her imagination was full of the dangers Mr.

16
00:01:02.708 --> 00:01:03.348
Fogg faced.

17
00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:07.132
At dawn the sun rose above a misty horizon.

18
00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:12.657
It was now possible to see objects two miles off, but the horizon was still vacant.

19
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:17.881
At seven o'clock the captain betrayed his worry and began to rub his hands anxiously.

20
00:01:19.162 --> 00:01:20.544
Then gunshots were heard.

21
00:01:21.444 --> 00:01:22.165
Was it a signal?

22
00:01:22.926 --> 00:01:25.088
The soldiers who had remained in the fort rushed out.

23
00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.121
Half a mile to the south, they could see a small group approaching.

24
00:01:30.062 --> 00:01:34.443
Mr. Fogg was riding at the head of the squad, and just behind him were Passepartout and the

25
00:01:34.503 --> 00:01:35.704
other two missing passengers.

26
00:01:36.984 --> 00:01:39.966
They had met and fought the Indians ten miles south of Fort Kearney.

27
00:01:42.126 --> 00:01:47.829
Passepartout and his companions had managed to escape their ropes and were already struggling against the Indians when

28
00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:49.489
Mr. Fogg and the soldiers arrived.

29
00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.150
The fight had been quick and successful.

30
00:01:53.191 --> 00:01:55.772
They were all welcomed back at the station with a joyful cheer.

31
00:01:57.368 --> 00:02:00.449
Phileas Fogg distributed the reward he had promised to the soldiers.

32
00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.832
Aouda took her protector's hand and pressed it to her own, too moved even to speak.

33
00:02:06.712 --> 00:02:09.133
Meanwhile Passepartout was looking around for the train.

34
00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:12.534
He thought he would find it there, ready to start for Omaha.

35
00:02:13.375 --> 00:02:15.336
He hoped that they could make up the lost time.

36
00:02:15.356 --> 00:02:17.517
"The train!

37
00:02:17.597 --> 00:02:18.117
The train!"

38
00:02:18.497 --> 00:02:19.437
cried Passepartout.

39
00:02:19.457 --> 00:02:21.658
"Gone," said Fix.

40
00:02:21.678 --> 00:02:24.299
"And when does the next train pass here?"

41
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.200
asked Mr.

42
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.629
Fogg. "Not until this evening," explained Aouda.

43
00:02:28.649 --> 00:02:30.749
"Ah," said Mr.

44
00:02:30.789 --> 00:02:31.569
Fogg quietly.

45
00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:35.651
They were now twenty hours behind schedule.

46
00:02:36.531 --> 00:02:37.451
Fix approached Mr.

47
00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:38.931
Fogg with a possible solution.

48
00:02:39.772 --> 00:02:44.113
While Fix had been sitting in the station, a man had approached him and offered him a ride

49
00:02:44.153 --> 00:02:45.333
on a sledge with sails.

50
00:02:46.373 --> 00:02:49.534
Fix pointed to the man, who was walking up and down in front of the station.

51
00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:52.195
Mr. Fogg paused.

52
00:02:52.935 --> 00:02:55.496
He had never heard of any vehicle like the one described.

53
00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.203
He went up to the man, and after a brief exchange, the two entered a hut a short

54
00:03:00.243 --> 00:03:01.184
distance from the fort.

55
00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:05.186
Mr. Fogg examined the curious device.

56
00:03:06.047 --> 00:03:09.209
A low frame was mounted on two long runners, like skis.

57
00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:14.033
A high mast was attached to the frame, held firmly by thin metal wires.

58
00:03:16.054 --> 00:03:17.936
Attached to the mast was a wide sail.

59
00:03:18.996 --> 00:03:24.132
The owner of the vehicle, a man named Mudge, said the sledge could take them to Omaha where

60
00:03:24.152 --> 00:03:27.175
they could catch a train to Chicago and finally to New York.

61
00:03:28.056 --> 00:03:29.717
Mr. Fogg made a bargain with Mudge.

62
00:03:30.979 --> 00:03:32.480
At eight o'clock the sledge was ready.

63
00:03:34.862 --> 00:03:38.325
Mr. Fogg, Aouda, Passepartout, and Fix, took their places.

64
00:03:39.406 --> 00:03:44.191
The great sail was hoisted, and soon they were skimming across the frozen prairie at forty miles an

65
00:03:44.231 --> 00:03:49.576
hour. The travelers huddled close together to protect themselves from the cold.

66
00:03:50.284 --> 00:03:53.766
The freezing wind burned Passepartout's face as red as the setting sun.

67
00:03:53.786 --> 00:03:56.547
Aouda was cozily wrapped in fur.

68
00:03:57.267 --> 00:04:01.269
Mr. Fogg was as calm and confident as usual, sitting close beside Aouda.

69
00:04:02.329 --> 00:04:04.970
Fix was happy to be moving closer to England with Mr.

70
00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:05.771
Fogg in his sight.

71
00:04:06.951 --> 00:04:09.112
Passepartout knew that he would never forget Mr.

72
00:04:09.152 --> 00:04:09.932
Fogg's bravery.

73
00:04:10.793 --> 00:04:12.673
Nor would he forget the sacrifice that Mr.

74
00:04:12.693 --> 00:04:14.754
Fogg had made to rescue him from the Indians.

75
00:04:15.495 --> 00:04:18.496
Without hesitation, Fogg had risked his fortune and his life!

76
00:04:19.868 --> 00:04:21.949
Mudge steered his strange vehicle skillfully.

77
00:04:22.869 --> 00:04:27.251
Traveling on skis, they were able to cross the frozen Platte River and shorten the distance they would

78
00:04:27.271 --> 00:04:28.191
have traveled by train.

79
00:04:29.152 --> 00:04:33.213
They arrived at the station in Omaha just in time to catch the next train to Chicago.

80
00:04:34.114 --> 00:04:38.696
Mr. Fogg generously rewarded Mudge, and they jumped aboard the train as it slowly pulled out of the

81
00:04:38.736 --> 00:04:45.658
station. What a strange way to travel!

82
00:04:46.559 --> 00:04:49.280
I've seen a sledge before, but I've never seen a sledge with sails!

83
00:04:50.344 --> 00:04:53.267
A sledge is a vehicle that slides along grass or snow.

84
00:04:54.288 --> 00:04:57.251
It does not have wheels, but instead has runners, which are like skis.

85
00:04:58.212 --> 00:05:01.815
A sledge can carry heavy things such as wood, stone, or blocks of ice.

86
00:05:02.896 --> 00:05:07.641
Many American farmers have sledges, which can be moved by people, animals, or even sails on a windy

87
00:05:07.681 --> 00:05:11.444
day! A sledge is similar to a sled or a sleigh.

88
00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:13.627
Have you ridden a sled in winter?

89
00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:14.888
It's fun, isn't it?

90
00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.224
A sled is smaller than a sledge or a sleigh and is usually used for fun or to

91
00:05:20.264 --> 00:05:21.065
carry a light load.

92
00:05:22.186 --> 00:05:24.669
Animals, like horses or dogs, or people can pull sleds.

93
00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:28.132
A sleigh is much larger and is usually pulled by horses.

94
00:05:28.913 --> 00:05:30.014
What a fabulous adventure!

95
00:05:30.654 --> 00:05:35.138
I never expected to find myself sailing— and then skiing—across the American prairies!
